/* ============================================================
   FODDER HP IS DEFINED IN SHOTS-TO-KILL (drop 0805b)

   Mike: "Normal - Recommended difficulty - 2-3 shots is norm across the board for most
   fodder enemies. 3-4 as you get to level 3 ... the max shots for an enemy except
   obstacles should be like 5-7 as fodder enemies."
   And: "Im thinking of slowed shots, so that might triple out with all numbers Idk.
   the dps is what matters I guess?"

   Yes — and that is exactly why this is expressed in SHOTS, not in HP. Raw HP is
   meaningless without knowing what a shot does; if the fire rate or pellet damage
   changes later, a shots-based table keeps the feel and only the derived HP moves.

   WHAT WAS ACTUALLY THERE. Measured, every type each stage really fields, at NORMAL,
   against the base gun (2 dmg/pellet):

       stage 1   intcp 2   topgun 2   racer 3   sandtank 3   jungletank 6
       stage 2   ash 7   skim 8   lance 8   eye 9   disc 11   cruc 23   carrier 31
       stage 3   minidrone 14
       stage 6   bcarrier 21   talon 53
       stage 8   oracle 14   hauler 17   talon 53   cdisc 60   hell 66

   Stage 2 is the fire stage. Its fodder took up to THIRTY-ONE shots, against a spec
   ceiling of seven — which is "fire enemies and all other fodder enemies in other
   levels are too tough/too much hp", measured.

   And there was NO STAGE SCALING AT ALL. DIFF.eHp is a single flat multiplier, so a
   stage-1 drone and a stage-8 drone had identical HP. The curve Mike is describing —
   2-3 early, 3-4 by stage 3, 5-7 at the end — did not exist in any form.

   HOW THE MAPPING WORKS. Authored base HP is kept as the RELATIVE weight (it encodes
   which units are meant to be tougher, and that judgement is the designer's). It is
   compressed onto the stage's shots band with a power curve, so ordering is preserved
   but the range is squeezed under the ceiling:

       shots = bandLo * (currentShots / 4) ^ 0.45      clamped to [1, bandHi]

   Pivot 2 and exponent 0.40 were MEASURED IN, not picked. Pivot 4 (the median fodder
   today) was tried first and put NORMAL stage 1 at 1-2 shots against a spec of 2-3 —
   the curve dragged light units under the band floor. Pivot 2 anchors a 2-shot unit
   exactly on the floor, so stage 1 reads 2-3 as specified, while genuinely trivial
   things still fall below it and a 66-shot sponge still lands on the cap.

   OBSTACLES ARE EXEMPT, per "the max shots for an enemy except obstacles". Anything
   flagged _obstacle keeps its authored HP untouched.
   BOSSES AND MINIBOSSES ARE EXEMPT — "mini bosses of course each get there own set of
   HP, and have there own way to defeat them." Their spawn sites do not route through
   here. ============================================================ */
const FODDER_BAND = {            // [lightest, heaviest] shots-to-kill at NORMAL
  1:[2,3], 2:[2,3], 3:[3,4], 4:[3,4], 5:[3,5], 6:[4,5], 7:[4,6], 8:[4,7], 9:[4,7],
};
const FODDER_DIFF = { easy:0.65, normal:1.0, hard:1.35, furious:1.60 };
const PLAYER_REF_DMG = 2;        // base MG pellet: _bdmg = 2 + floor(lv/2), so 2 at L0/L1
function fodderShots(baseHp, stage, dkey){
  const band = FODDER_BAND[stage] || FODDER_BAND[8];
  const cur  = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(baseHp / PLAYER_REF_DMG));   // what it costs today
  let s = band[0] * Math.pow(cur / 2, 0.40);
  s = Math.min(band[1], Math.max(1, s));
  s *= (FODDER_DIFF[dkey!=null?dkey:diffKey] || 1);
  return Math.max(1, Math.round(s));
}
/* THE ONE CHOKEPOINT every fodder spawn goes through. Replaces 43 separate
   `EHP(N)` expressions, which is how the flat multiplier managed to be
   the only scaling in the game for this long — there was no single place to change it. */
function EHP(baseHp){
  const stage = (typeof run!=='undefined' && run && run.stage) ? run.stage : 1;
  return Math.max(1, fodderShots(baseHp, stage, diffKey) * PLAYER_REF_DMG);
}

